// A HOLDER OF THE OWNER HANDED TO A CALL BESIDE A VIEW THAT IS STILL LIVE (N and K of landing 177a; management_12's rulings of 2026-11-01 10:31 and 10:55). `W:w = W{ p: @s }; fs(s, (raw wh(w)));` copied `s`'s header for the outer call,
// the nested call wrote through the holder and freed the bytes, and the outer callee read them (83, the freed bytes reused, on 175 and 176b; also through a pointer to the holder, a ternary, a literal, an array element, a copy, a holder a call made,
// a held view, a laundered view). The holder rule read the views of ITS OWN call; now `rejudge_holder` judges every LIVE view at every call, whole, the summary unread. A view held BY the holder itself is that holder's own storage (the twin in `holder_beside_live_view_ok.npk`, `ks5_self_holder_ok`).
// OVER-REFUSED ON PURPOSE, THE POST-180 LIST: a callee that only reads (`h20_read_nested`, `h22_view_read_ok`), and a laundered view beside a holder of another field (`la3_laundered_field`, 127 and safe on 176b).
// K: a value holding an address INTO ITSELF, `o.p = @o.s; selfw(o)`, handed by value: the callee writes through the copy's pointer and reads the copy's freed `s` (`k_self`, 83). A whole-self address (`o.me = @o; selfw(o)`, `k2_whole`, 83) stays OPEN, on DEF-418's row: it is accepted and reads freed bytes.
mod:holder_beside_live_view;
error:E5;
